// cppish: C plus the one thing that makes C++ hard to parse.
//
// `a < b > c;` is either the expression `(a < b) > c` or a declaration of
// `c` with type `a<b>`, and nothing short of a symbol table decides it.
// SDF3 parses both and keeps them; `{prefer}` picks the template reading
// when both survive to the end of the statement. treebank-cpp carries the
// same ambiguity as declared conflicts, and cut template arguments in
// EXPRESSION position (`f<int>(x)`) to keep its table converging -- see its
// ledger. This module keeps them in TYPE position only, as that grammar
// does.
//
// The module IMPORTS cish rather than copying it: SDF3 composition is
// additive, so `Type` gains a production and every other rule is cish's.
// `>>` stays a single token in cish; `vector<vector<int>> v;` must still
// parse, which tree-sitter's state-aware lexer settles without a scanner.

module cppish

imports cish

context-free syntax
  Type.TemplateId = <<name:ID>\<<arguments:{Type ","}+>\>> {prefer}
